From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dugs <dugahol@protonmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8015-8plus: Leave the AOP GPIO interrupts
 to the AOP

The AOP firmware owns the interrupts of its own GPIO block. On the
iPhone 8 Plus the Touch ID sensor's interrupt is AOP GPIO 21 (group 0),
and the AOP arms it once the AP releases the sensor (Mesa command 0x21
after 0x20).

The t8015 dtsi makes pinctrl_aop an interrupt controller with all seven
group lines (AIC 135-141), so Linux takes them as chained handlers on
CPU0. Nothing on the 8 Plus requests an AOP GPIO interrupt, so the pin
has no mapping: apple_gpio_irq_handler() dispatches into nothing, nobody
acks it, and the level line fires again at once. CPU0 never leaves the
handler and stops answering IPIs; the RCU stall detector names it, and
everything routed to CPU0 (SMC, Wi-Fi, USB networking) times out.

The same happens if the AOP crashes with a pin pending. Before, the
handover locked the phone up about 30 s later; without the interrupt
controller on this board it ran past 80 s, the AOP kept reporting, and
the phone stayed reachable.
The iPhone X keeps it; its touchscreen does use an AOP GPIO interrupt.

Tested on the iPhone 8 Plus (D21). D211 is the same board.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dugs <dugahol@protonmail.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8015-8plus.dtsi | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8015-8plus.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8015-8plus.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8015-8plus.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8015-8plus.dtsi
@@ -7,3 +7,16 @@
 
 /* The 8 Plus has minor differences like 1 more camera, 1 GiB of RAM more and a bigger display. */
 #include "t8015-8.dtsi"
+
+/*
+ * The AOP owns the interrupts of its own GPIOs: it arms the Touch ID
+ * sensor's interrupt (AOP GPIO 21) once the AP hands the sensor over.
+ * Nothing here takes an AOP GPIO interrupt, and if Linux claims the
+ * group lines it sees that pin pending forever and CPU0 never leaves
+ * the handler.
+ */
+&pinctrl_aop {
+	/delete-property/ interrupt-controller;
+	/delete-property/ #interrupt-cells;
+	/delete-property/ interrupts;
+};
